"1. Indian J Pharmacol. 2026 Sep 1;58(5):530-536. doi: 10.4103/ijp.ijp_1239_25.\nEpub  2026 Sep 2.\n\nKnowledge, awareness, and practices of adverse drug reaction reporting among \nhealthcare professionals in the Ashanti region of Ghana.\n\nFiagbey EDK(1)(2), Nyame L(3), Bao Z(1)(2), Kipanga ZG(1)(2), Lamptey INL(4), \nNyame DK(1), Dekyem H(1), Maigari YN(5), Abdon AE(1)(2), Thomford KP(6), Zou \nJ(7).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)School of Basic Medicine and Clinical Pharmacy, China Pharmaceutical \nUniversity, Jiangsu, China.\n(2)Department of Pharmacy, Nanjing First Hospital, Jiangsu, China.\n(3)School of Public Health, Southeast University, Jiangsu, China.\n(4)School of Medical Sciences, University of Cape Coast, Ghana.\n(5)Department of Pharmaceutics, School of Pharmacy, China Pharmaceutical \nUniversity, Jiangsu, China.\n(6)Department of Pharmacognosy and Herbal Medicine, University of Cape Coast, \nCentral Region, Ghana.\n(7)Department of Pharmacy, Nanjing First Hospital, Nanjing Medical University \nNanjing, Jiangsu, China.\n\nOBJECTIVES: To assess the knowledge, awareness, and practices of adverse drug \nreaction (ADR) reporting among healthcare professionals (HCPs) in the Ashanti \nRegion of Ghana and to further identify the predictors of HCPs' reporting \nbehaviors.\nMETHODS: A cross-sectional survey was conducted among 326 HCPs from various \nhealthcare facilities across the Ashanti Region. Data were collected using \nstructured, pretested questionnaires, and analyzed with R and STATA software, \nusing descriptive statistics and univariate logistic regression to explore the \nfactors associated with reporting behaviors.\nRESULTS: 93.6% of HCPs were aware of pharmacovigilance (PV), with 85.6% \nacknowledging ADR reporting as a professional mandate. However, only 49.4% were \naware of Ghana's National Pharmacovigilance Center, with only 50% having \nsubmitted fully-filled ADR forms. Barriers to reporting included unavailability \nof forms (30.3%) and lack of training (23.3%). Age, experience, and previous \ntraining significantly influenced reporting (P < 0.05). The most ADR-implicated \ndrugs were antibiotics (36.5%) and antihypertensives (17.8%), with \ngastrointestinal (34.4%), and central nervous system (32.4%) manifestations most \noften reported.\nCONCLUSIONS: This study reveals relevant insights into the PV landscape in \nGhana's Ashanti Region. Despite high awareness among HCPs in the Ashanti Region, \noperational knowledge and reporting practice were suboptimal, with most cited \nbarriers being unavailability of forms and lack of training. Our findings \nhighlight the need for targeted interventions such as continuous PV training, \nimproved access to reporting tools, and inclusion of underrepresented HCP cadres \nlike Medical Herbalists to strengthen PV and improve ADR reporting in Ghana.\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2026 Indian Journal of Pharmacology.\n\nDOI: 10.4103/ijp.ijp_1239_25\nPMID: 42683983 [Indexed for MEDLINE]"